WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.267 --> 00:00:05.873
"How Well Do GLP-1 Drugs Like Ozempic
(Semaglutide) Work for Weight Loss?"

00:00:07.307 --> 00:00:09.109
Older weight-loss
medications achieve

00:00:09.109 --> 00:00:11.411
approximately 5 percent
reduction in body weight

00:00:11.411 --> 00:00:12.746
over placebo,

00:00:12.746 --> 00:00:15.616
whereas these new GLP-1
agonists, like Ozempic,

00:00:15.616 --> 00:00:18.518
produce about triple the effect,
about 15 percent.

00:00:18.518 --> 00:00:22.489
But what does that mean in terms
of numbers on the scale?

00:00:22.489 --> 00:00:25.158
In an analysis of more than
100 clinical trials

00:00:25.158 --> 00:00:27.828
of earlier anti-obesity
medications,

00:00:27.828 --> 00:00:30.364
in studies lasting
up to 76 weeks,

00:00:30.364 --> 00:00:32.699
drug-induced weight loss
never exceeded

00:00:32.699 --> 00:00:35.669
more than 9 pounds (4 kg).

00:00:35.669 --> 00:00:38.672
If you put all the GLP-1 drug
trials together,

00:00:38.672 --> 00:00:40.107
over an average of about a year,

00:00:40.107 --> 00:00:44.044
they led to about 19 pounds
(8 kg) of weight loss.

00:00:44.044 --> 00:00:47.481
And that folded in
some of the older GLP-1 drugs,

00:00:47.481 --> 00:00:49.983
like liraglutide,
that don’t work as well.

00:00:49.983 --> 00:00:51.618
If you just look at semaglutide,

00:00:51.618 --> 00:00:55.222
the high-dose Ozempic
sold as Wegovy for weight loss,

00:00:55.222 --> 00:00:59.092
we’re looking at more like
27 pounds (12 kg) lost,

00:00:59.092 --> 00:01:01.561
which translates into about
three and a half inches (9 cm)

00:01:01.561 --> 00:01:03.297
off the waist.

00:01:03.297 --> 00:01:07.067
Which GLP-1 drug works best?

00:01:07.067 --> 00:01:09.736
An oral version of Ozempic
has been developed.

00:01:09.736 --> 00:01:12.072
Though it hasn’t been approved
for weight loss,

00:01:12.072 --> 00:01:14.975
preliminary evidence suggests
it causes similar weight loss

00:01:14.975 --> 00:01:17.077
to the injectable version.

00:01:17.077 --> 00:01:20.881
The latest GLP-1 drug approved
for weight loss, tirzepatide,

00:01:20.881 --> 00:01:24.251
sold as Zepbound,
appears to beat out semaglutide,

00:01:24.251 --> 00:01:26.887
with nearly 19 percent
of body weight lost,

00:01:26.887 --> 00:01:28.355
over 40 pounds (18 kg),

00:01:28.355 --> 00:01:31.792
with more like five and a half
inches (14 cm) off the waist.

00:01:31.792 --> 00:01:34.695
How does that compare
to bariatric surgery?

00:01:34.695 --> 00:01:36.530
It’s actually not that far off

00:01:36.530 --> 00:01:39.032
from long-term
surgical weight loss.

00:01:39.032 --> 00:01:41.501
Now, remember,
these are averages.

00:01:41.501 --> 00:01:44.171
Bariatric surgery
doesn’t always work.

00:01:44.171 --> 00:01:46.606
The failure rate
for the gold standard procedure

00:01:46.606 --> 00:01:48.275
is approximately 20 percent,

00:01:48.275 --> 00:01:52.312
but not everyone responds
to the GLP-1 drugs either.

00:01:52.312 --> 00:01:53.580
In the big Ozempic trials,

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:57.017
about a third of people were
"super responders,"

00:01:57.017 --> 00:02:00.520
but as many as one in six didn’t
lose significant weight at all,

00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:02.889
even after taking the drug
for more than a year.

00:02:02.889 --> 00:02:05.892
So, despite impressive results
at a population level,

00:02:05.892 --> 00:02:07.661
up to 20 percent of patients

00:02:07.661 --> 00:02:09.429
fail to achieve
significant weight loss

00:02:09.429 --> 00:02:12.799
with either surgical
or drug-based approaches.

00:02:12.799 --> 00:02:14.868
Here’s the longest trial
to date,

00:02:14.868 --> 00:02:17.471
four years of high-dose Ozempic
for weight loss.

00:02:17.471 --> 00:02:20.273
Here’s how the spread looked
at the end of Year Two,

00:02:20.273 --> 00:02:23.276
with each of the thousands
of participants lined up

00:02:23.276 --> 00:02:25.379
from those losing
the most weight

00:02:25.379 --> 00:02:26.980
to the those losing the least.

00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:29.650
The average weight loss was
about 10 percent;

00:02:29.650 --> 00:02:32.319
so, some lost even more weight,

00:02:32.319 --> 00:02:34.488
and sometimes,
a lot more weight,

00:02:34.488 --> 00:02:36.490
some lost less,

00:02:36.490 --> 00:02:38.392
and some even gained weight,

00:02:38.392 --> 00:02:40.060
as much as maybe 40 pounds
(18 kg),

00:02:40.060 --> 00:02:43.997
even after continuing
to inject the drug for years.

00:02:43.997 --> 00:02:46.266
Here’s the average
weight-loss curve

00:02:46.266 --> 00:02:48.835
of those getting high-dose
Ozempic for four years,

00:02:48.835 --> 00:02:51.438
versus those getting
placebo injections,

00:02:51.438 --> 00:02:55.475
for an overall weight loss
over placebo of about 9 percent.

00:02:55.475 --> 00:03:01.048
OK, but the most striking thing
about this graph is the plateau.

00:03:01.048 --> 00:03:04.284
They were being injected with
the drug the whole four years,

00:03:04.284 --> 00:03:07.554
but weight loss stalled
after only about a year.

00:03:07.554 --> 00:03:15.062
This happened in study after
study after study after study,

00:03:15.062 --> 00:03:19.866
with both Ozempic
and the newer tirzepatide.

00:03:19.866 --> 00:03:22.169
So, even under
the best of circumstances,

00:03:22.169 --> 00:03:24.638
with the most potent GLP-1 drugs

00:03:24.638 --> 00:03:26.339
taken at the highest
allowable dose

00:03:26.339 --> 00:03:29.209
for at least a year
to obtain the full weight loss,

00:03:29.209 --> 00:03:32.012
we’re only looking at
a 34-pound (15 kg) drop

00:03:32.012 --> 00:03:34.347
before the weight loss stops.

00:03:34.347 --> 00:03:36.116
So, if you look at all three

00:03:36.116 --> 00:03:37.584
of the original
placebo-controlled

00:03:37.584 --> 00:03:39.486
Ozempic weight loss trials,

00:03:39.486 --> 00:03:42.255
the participants
started out obese,

00:03:42.255 --> 00:03:44.324
and after more than a year
on the drug

00:03:44.324 --> 00:03:46.259
(when it effectively
stopped working),

00:03:46.259 --> 00:03:48.929
they were still obese.

00:03:48.929 --> 00:03:52.299
They were certainly less obese,
but still obese.

00:03:52.299 --> 00:03:55.669
Then, to keep yourself
at that same level of obese,

00:03:55.669 --> 00:03:57.337
you still have
to keep injecting it

00:03:57.337 --> 00:03:59.072
at, like, $1,000 a month,

00:03:59.072 --> 00:04:03.210
or you’ll go back to your
original level of obese.

00:04:03.210 --> 00:04:05.979
But why does
the weight loss plateau,

00:04:05.979 --> 00:04:09.349
and what exactly happens
when you do stop the drugs?

00:04:09.349 --> 00:04:13.186
I’ll cover both those questions
next.

